::ingredients

(rice) 1/4lb
(butter) 1/4lb
(sugar) 1/4lb
(milk choice) = (milk) 1 1/2 pints -OR- (cream and milk) 1 1/2 pints
(egg) 6
(mixed spice, mace and nutmeg and cinnamon) 1 tea-spoonful
(rose-water) 1/2 wine-glass
(nutmeg, for top) as needed
(fruit choice, optional) = (currants) as needed -OR- (raisins) as needed
(flour, for optional fruit) as needed
(water, for washing and boiling rice) as needed

::equipment

(pan)
(oven)

::instructions

(rice) <wash> <boil, until very soft> <drain> <cool>
{butter mixture} = (butter) + (sugar) <stir, pan, until very light>
    + (mixed spice) + (rose-water) <add>
{egg mixture} = (egg) <beat, very light>
    + (milk choice) <stir, gradually>
{butter mixture} + {egg mixture} + (rice) <stir, alternate egg mixture with rice>
Optional [
    (fruit choice) + (flour) <coat> <stir, into pudding at last>
]
{pudding} <bake, temperature and duration not stated>
    + (nutmeg) <grate, over top>
    <cool> <serve, cold or quite cool>

::notes
It should be eaten cold, or quite cool.

::source
<<<
---
title: "Rice Pudding"
tags: ['rice', 'dessert', 'pudding']
date: 1832-01-01
author: "Miss Leslie"
---

## Ingredients

- A quarter of a pound of rice.
- A quarter of a pound of butter.
- A quarter of a pound of sugar.
- A pint and a half of milk, or cream and milk.
- Six eggs.
- A tea-spoonful of mixed spice, mace, nutmeg and cinnamon.
- A half wine-glass of rose-water.

## Directions

1. Wash the rice. Boil it till very soft. Drain it and set it away to get cold. Put the butter and sugar together in a pan, and stir them till very light. Add to them the spice and rose-water. Beat the eggs very light, and stir them, gradually, into the milk. Then stir the eggs and the milk into the butter and sugar, alternately with the rice.
2. Bake it and grate nutmeg over the top.
3. Currants or raisins, floured, and stirred in at the last, will greatly improve it.

It should be eaten cold, or quite cool.
>>>
